WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.870
While it's easily one of the most enjoyable
installments in the MCU, Avengers: Endgame

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:06.280
is anything but straightforward.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.460
Filled with time travel, space aliens, doppelgangers
from the past, and a whole mess of magic space

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.480
rocks, there are plenty of moments in the
film that are simply confusing.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.400
Fortunately, if there's any part of the movie
that makes you scratch your head, just wait

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.160
five minutes.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.140
Chances are good someone involved in the production
will give an interview that attempts to straighten

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:23.640
it all out.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:28.340
Case in point, sibling filmmakers Joe and
Anthony Russo got candid with Business Insider

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:32.920
about the Endgame scene featuring a past version
of the otherwise dead Norse God Loki.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.300
While Iron Man, Ant-Man, Professor Hulk, and
Captain America return to New York City in

00:00:37.309 --> 00:00:42.489
2012 to collect the Tesseract and Loki's scepter,
which held the Space Stone and the Mind Stone,

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:46.780
respectively, Loki swiftly grabs the Tesseract
and escapes Thor's custody through a portal.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.700
Once he's gone, that's pretty much the last
anyone sees of Loki in Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:51.760
So, what went down?

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:52.760
Where is Loki now?

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:57.729
According to co-director Joe Russo, Loki is
still dead in the core Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:00:57.729 --> 00:01:02.090
timeline; his capturing of the Space Stone
in 2012 doesn't reverse his Infinity War death

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:03.340
at the hands of Thanos.

00:01:03.569 --> 00:01:06.160
But he is alive in a different pocket of the
multiverse.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:09.140
Loki snatching the Stone and beaming away
created a unique timeline.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.390
That more or less falls in line with the conversation
between Professor Hulk and the Ancient One,

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:15.680
when the two met to discuss him borrowing
the Time Stone.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.280
Essentially, Loki's escape with the Space
Stone was significant enough to cause the

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.300
central timeline to break off into a branched
reality.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.760
The filmmaker explained:

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:28.140
"Loki, when he teleports away with the [Space]
Stone, would create his own timeline.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.189
It gets very complicated, but it would be
impossible for [Cap] to rectify the timeline

00:01:32.189 --> 00:01:33.390
unless he found Loki.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.380
The minute that Loki does something as dramatic
as take the Space Stone, he creates a branched

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:38.060
reality."

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.070
Now, Tony Stark and Steve Rogers did wind
up getting their hands on the Space Stone

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:47.400
by utilizing the last of their available Pym
Particles to travel to 1970, steal the Space

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.549
Stone from S.H.I.E.L.D., and then snag a few
more vials of Particles from Hank Pym's lab

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:52.880
so they could return home again.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.900
This allowed them to fulfill their time heist
mission and load up their own Infinity Gauntlet

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:01.100
in the year 2023 to revive the heroes who
were killed at the end of Infinity War.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.439
But that didn't fix the problem of there being
a Loki somewhere out there with the Space

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:05.880
Stone he stole in 2012.

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:10.220
The final moments of Endgame saw Cap returning
all six Infinity Stones to their rightful

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:11.320
places and times.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.300
And in the case of the Space Stone, he took
that one back to the S.H.I.E.L.D. base in

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.960
1970, not to New York in 2012.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.860
It's confusing, like Russo mentioned, but
the truth of the matter is that taking the

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:27.870
Tesseract from and returning it to 1970 doesn't
affect Loki snagging it in 2012, at least

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.880
not according to the time travel standards
by which Endgame operates.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.400
"Somebody peed my pants."

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:33.500
"Time travel!"

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:37.540
As of now, past Loki is living it up with
the Space Stone in an alternate timeline.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.300
He isn't the same character fans saw try to
kind of redeem himself in Thor: The Dark World.

00:02:42.301 --> 00:02:45.790
He isn't the same one who fought Hela's army
in Thor: Ragnarok and then die in Avengers:

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:46.840
Infinity War, either.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.260
This is pre-redemption arc Loki, a Loki who
is still a villain who tried to ravage Earth

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:52.560
on behalf of Thanos.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.160
The door is open for this Loki to return to
the Marvel Cinematic Universe in a future

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.830
film, but it's more likely that evil 2012
Loki will first rear his head on his standalone

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.600
streaming series headed to Disney+.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:07.820
Marvel Studios president Kevin Feige has noted
that the Disney+ superhero series, which also

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:12.430
include WandaVision and a Winter Soldier/Falcon
team-up show, will, quote, "intersect with

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:16.959
the movies in a very big way," suggesting
that Loki's sneaky getaway in Endgame could

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.500
shape everything about his forthcoming TV
series.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:23.790
It's unclear when the project will hit the
service, so fans have plenty of time to speculate

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:26.620
about how the series will explore Loki's alternate
timeline.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.020
Is he being an all-out baddie and asking strangers
to bend the knee to him?

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:33.640
Or is he taking a more subtle approach to
villainy, kicking back while he chills his

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:34.820
butt on a throne somewhere?

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:39.660
Either seem plausible, and plot points we'd
be down to see unfold on the Loki series.

